# Reading Surface Rule

Human-facing knowledge artifacts in this repo need a readable surface.

Canonical data and source metadata must remain complete in Markdown or other
structured source files. The reading surface should make that material easier to
read, revisit, index, and cross-link; it must not replace or compress away the
canonical record.

The current default reading surface under `research/knowledge/**` is
self-contained HTML. The goal is not HTML/CSS for its own sake. If a future
repo-supported rendered format gives equal or better readability, navigation,
traceability, and maintainability, use that format intentionally.

Follow [docs/HTML_READING_UI_GUIDE.md](mdc:docs/HTML_READING_UI_GUIDE.md).

## Required Behavior

- Preserve canonical data completeness. Do not make the reading page the only
  place where facts, source metadata, or review state live.
- If a user is expected to read, learn, review, or revisit an artifact, create or
  update a reading surface. Self-contained HTML is the current default for
  `research/knowledge/**`.
- Use the reading surface to improve navigation: indexes, breadcrumbs,
  next-reading links, and links between related artifacts.
- Prefer rendered HTML links inside HTML pages.
- Markdown links are allowed only as fallback and must be explicitly labeled:
  `md source`, `markdown intake`, or `markdown canonical`.
- Do not label a Markdown link as just `source`, `intake`, or `Q&A`.
- If no specific HTML page exists, link to the closest HTML reading view first.

## Required Page Structure

Every human-facing HTML reading page should include:

- Breadcrumb.
- Clear H1 title.
- One-paragraph summary / lede.
- Status or source-quality metadata.
- Short, scannable sections.
- Source confidence near claims.
- Follow-up / unknowns when applicable.
- Rendered reading links and clearly labeled canonical fallback links.

## Style Constraints

- Static, self-contained HTML is preferred for the current knowledge pages.
- Inline CSS is preferred while the repo has no shared reading renderer.
- Avoid framework or asset dependencies unless the workflow explicitly supports
  them.
- No external assets.
- No JavaScript unless a later milestone explicitly requests interaction.
- No gradients, decorative animations, heavy shadows, or emoji icons.
- Keep it readable on mobile widths.

## Review Checklist

Before committing:

- [ ] Can the page be understood without opening Markdown?
- [ ] Does the canonical Markdown or structured source still preserve the full
      record?
- [ ] Are primary / secondary / unverified claims clearly labeled?
- [ ] Are high-risk claims accompanied by plain-language warnings?
- [ ] Are Markdown links explicitly fallback links?
- [ ] Is the page useful for re-reading, not just visually nicer?
